# Step 94 (monitor/server.py became the package monitor/server/: the app and its state in application.py, the
# helpers every route uses in support.py, one module per route group, the /ws push in websocket.py). The route
# modules register on the one `app` of application.py, and every piece of state that `configure()` rebinds lives
# there, so the imports run one way only: websocket, then the route modules (independent of each other; the one
# exception is the push sweeping the builder sessions, which is a higher layer reading a lower one), then support,
# then application. A route module that imported another, or application importing a route, would be a cycle at
# import time (the registration order is the import order) and a bound copy of state `configure()` cannot reach.
[importlinter:contract:monitor-server-layers]
name = the modules of fsm_llm.monitor.server import only downward: websocket, route modules, support, application
type = layers
layers =
    fsm_llm.monitor.server.websocket
    fsm_llm.monitor.server.routes_agent | fsm_llm.monitor.server.routes_builder | fsm_llm.monitor.server.routes_fsm | fsm_llm.monitor.server.routes_monitoring | fsm_llm.monitor.server.routes_visualize | fsm_llm.monitor.server.routes_workflow
    fsm_llm.monitor.server.support
    fsm_llm.monitor.server.application
